auth {
  mode: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

tests {
  // Runs after EVERY request in this collection.
  test("the custom middleware timed the request", function() {
    expect(res.getHeader("x-process-time-ms")).to.be.a("string");
    expect(Number(res.getHeader("x-process-time-ms"))).to.be.a("number");
  });
}

docs {
  # HR Skills REST API

  Eight endpoints over one in-memory store: a directory of employees, a set of open
  requisitions, and a scoring engine that decides who fits. Start the server first:

      cd fast-api
      uv run app/main.py          # port 8100 — what the Local environment expects

  Then pick the **Local** environment (top right) and run the collection top to bottom.

  > Running `uv run uvicorn app.main:app --reload` instead serves port **8000**. Both are
  > in the README; the Local environment tracks `app/main.py` because that is what the
  > repo's VS Code launch config starts. Change `baseUrl` if you use the other command.

  ## What separates this from the GraphQL collection

  Its sibling in [`fast-api-graphql/bruno`](../../fast-api-graphql/bruno) answers **200** to
  everything and puts the outcome in the body. Here the **status line carries the meaning**,
  and each code means something different:

  | Code | Means |
  |---|---|
  | `201` | created — a new employee, or a new shortlist entry |
  | `404` | you named something that does not exist |
  | `409` | your request was fine; the *state* refuses it — a blocked candidate |
  | `422` | the payload never got past Pydantic |

  The `409` is the one worth reading twice. It is deliberately not a `400`: that distinction
  is what lets a client tell *"you sent me nonsense"* apart from *"this person genuinely
  cannot do the job"*.

  The only test that runs everywhere is the middleware assertion above — every response,
  success or failure, carries `X-Process-Time-ms`, because the middleware wraps the whole
  stack rather than the happy path.

  ## Order matters

  Folders run in the `seq` recorded in each `folder.bru`: **Meta → Employees →
  Requisitions → Matching → Shortlist → Errors**. *Employees* creates a person that
  *Matching* then has to tolerate in the rankings, and *Shortlist* adds an entry before
  *Read the shortlist* checks it.

  The collection is **re-runnable against a long-lived server**. Shortlisting is idempotent,
  the employee it creates is deliberately too weak to displace anyone in a top-3, and every
  membership assertion uses `include` rather than an exact count.
}
